# text_id = 1967
# text = THE EARL OF ELLENBOROUGH said , he understood the general rule to be this — that no public officer who had left office was at liberty to make use publicly of any document which had come into his possession while in office , except it had been published ; and no Minister was at liberty to read anything from a public document , without at once laying it on the table .
